</funding-source></award-group></funding-group><counts><fig-count count="5"/><table-count count="0"/><page-count count="20"/><word-count count="14400"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>04 November 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.6 mode:remove_FC converted:21.11.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><fn-group id="jah311546-ntgp-0001"><fn id="jah311546-note-0001"><p>This article was sent to Chad E. Grueter, PhD, Associate Editor, for review by expert referees, editorial decision, and final disposition.</p></fn><fn id="jah311546-note-0002"><p>Preprint posted on BioRxiv February 23, 2025. doi: <ext-link xlink:href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.02.18.638845" ext-link-type="uri">https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.02.18.638845</ext-link>.</p></fn><fn id="jah311546-note-0003"><p>Supplemental Material is available at <ext-link xlink:href="https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/suppl/10.1161/JAHA.125.043646" ext-link-type="uri">https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/suppl/10.1161/JAHA.125.043646</ext-link>
</p></fn><fn id="jah311546-note-0004"><p>For Sources of Funding: and Disclosures, see page 18.</p></fn></fn-group></notes></front><body id="jah311546-body-0001"><def-list list-content="abbreviations" id="jah311546-dl-0001"><title>Nonstandard Abbreviations and Acronyms</title><def-item><term id="jah311546-li-0001">FBS</term><def id="jah311546-li-0002"><p>fetal bovine serum</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="jah311546-li-0003">moMac</term><def id="jah311546-li-0004"><p>monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophage</p></def></def-item><def-item><term id="jah311546-li-0005">oxLDL</term><def id="jah311546-li-0006"><p>oxidized low&#x02010;density lipoprotein</p></def></def-item></def-list><p>
<boxed-text position="anchor" content-type="box" id="jah311546-blkfxd-0001"><caption><title>Research Perspective</title></caption><sec id="jah311546-sec-0007"><title>What Is New?</title><p>
<list list-type="bullet" id="jah311546-list-0001"><list-item id="jah311546-li-0007"><p>The hematopoietic transcription factor GATA2 was found to be highly upregulated in macrophages within human atherosclerotic plaque across a range of disease severities; here, GATA2 expression correlated with expression of the proliferation maker Ki&#x02010;67.</p></list-item><list-item id="jah311546-li-0008"><p>Transcriptomic analysis of these macrophages identified the upregulation of several proliferation&#x02010;associated genes; GATA2 was found to be expressed much less frequently in macrophages present in other tissues in these patients, with no correlation between GATA2 expression and the expression of proliferation markers observed in these nonatheroma macrophages.</p></list-item><list-item id="jah311546-li-0009"><p>In vitro, GATA2 expression was induced in patient&#x02010;derived and cell&#x02010;line macrophages by oxidized low&#x02010;density lipoprotein, where GATA2 was necessary and sufficient to induce proliferation; this GATA2&#x02010;dependent proliferation occurred primarily during monocyte&#x02010;to&#x02010;macrophage differentiation and not during macrophage&#x02010;to&#x02010;foam cell polarization.</p></list-item></list>
</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0008"><title>What Question Should Be Addressed Next?</title><p>
<list list-type="bullet" id="jah311546-list-0002"><list-item id="jah311546-li-0010"><p>Although we identified oxidized low&#x02010;density lipoprotein as a stimulus that induces GATA2, identification of the signaling pathways and transcription factors that mediate the upregulation of GATA2 in atheroma macrophages is a priority, as these pathways may present new therapeutic targets for reducing the cellularity of atherosclerotic plaque.</p></list-item></list>
</p></sec></boxed-text>
</p><p>Macrophages are the primary cell type involved in the development of atherosclerosis, where they serve both protective and atherogenic roles.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>1</sup>
</xref> In early disease, oxidized low&#x02010;density lipoprotein (oxLDL) and other modified forms of LDL are deposited within the intima of the vasculature. These deposits are engulfed by macrophages via the scavenger receptor CD36, and then the cholesterol is exported to high&#x02010;density lipoprotein (HDL), thereby removing the deposits from the vascular intima and returning the material to the circulation.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>2</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>3</sup>
</xref> Excess uptake of oxLDL can overwhelm this system, causing macrophages to accumulate cholesterol and lipids in intracellular droplets, forming foam cells.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref> Foam cells are highly inflammatory and promote the ingress of monocytes which differentiate into additional macrophages. These foam cells eventually die due to activation of the unfolded protein response that is induced by the intracellular stress of these accumulated lipids and sterols.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>5</sup>
</xref> Normally, other macrophages would clear these dying cells through efferocytosis, but for unclear reasons this activity is lost early in disease.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>6</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>7</sup>
</xref> The loss of efferocytosis results in the accumulation of apoptotic foam cells, which uncleared, undergo secondary necrosis and spill their accumulated lipid and sterol content back into the extracellular space.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>8</sup>
</xref> This accumulation of dead macrophages and the cell&#x02010;free lipids and sterols released from these cells forms the necrotic core that typifies atherosclerotic lesions. In early disease most plaque macrophages are derived from monocytes recruited from the vasculature, but as disease progresses the growth of the plaque macrophage population becomes increasingly dependent on macrophage proliferation.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>In mouse models, plaque macrophages undergo turnover in just 4&#x02009;weeks, with local proliferation accounting for nearly 90% of macrophage replenishment in advanced plaques.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref> Consistent with this, targeting macrophage proliferation is sufficient to reduce plaque inflammation and promote plaque remodeling to a less pathological state.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref> In fact, some studies suggest that the antiproliferative impact of statins may account for the majority of their impact on plaque regression, exceeding the contribution of statins on plaque inflammation and lowering cholesterol levels.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>11</sup>
</xref> Plaque macrophage proliferation is initiated by oxLDL&#x02010;induced scavenger receptor signaling, which then activates a downstream signaling cascade dependent on AMPK (5&#x02019; AMP&#x02010;activated protein kinase), PI3K (phosphatidylinositol 3&#x02010;kinases), p38 MAPK (mitogen&#x02010;activated protein kinase), and ERK (extracellular signal&#x02010;regulated kinases).<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>12</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>13</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>14</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>15</sup>
</xref> This cascade is further amplified by oxLDL&#x02010;induced autocrine signaling via M&#x02010;CSF (macrophage colony&#x02010;stimulating factor) and GM&#x02010;CSF (granulocyte M&#x02010;CSF).<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>16</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>17</sup>
</xref> But although the ligands and upstream signaling molecules mediating this macrophage proliferation are established, the specific transcriptional targets and cell cycle pathways involved in this process remain largely unknown.</p><p>We recently identified a population of GATA2&#x02010;expressing macrophages in small, early&#x02010;stage plaques found in the intima of aortic biopsies recovered during coronary artery bypass graft surgery (CABG).<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> This population of macrophages accounted for a large portion of the macrophages present in these lesions and in&#x000a0;vitro exhibited several atherogenic phenotypes including impaired cholesterol export and defects in apoptotic cell removal and processing.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> Finding GATA2 expression in these cells was unexpected, as previous literature suggested that GATA2 expression in myeloid cells is limited to the bone marrow (reviewed in<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref>). Within the bone marrow, GATA2 is required for the proliferation of hematopoietic stem cells and myeloid precursor cells before their commitment to terminal differentiation.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>20</sup>
</xref> Indeed, the only other context in which myeloid cells regularly express GATA2 outside of the bone marrow niche is in myeloid leukemia and myelodysplastic syndrome, where GATA2 expression promotes cell cycle progression and malignancy.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>19</sup>
</xref> GATA2 is a member of the GATA family of transcription factors and binds to the DNA motif &#x0201c;GATA&#x0201d; via a tandem zinc finger motif.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>21</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>22</sup>
</xref> GATA2 can function as a pioneering transcription factor, opening heterochromatin to form euchromatin that is accessible to other transcription factors and RNA polymerase.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>23</sup>
</xref> In bone marrow, GATA2 expression is required for the self&#x02010;renewal of hematopoietic stem cells and hematopoietic progenitor cells. GATA2 expression peaks when these cells commit to the myeloid lineage, with GATA2 expression then decreasing during terminal differentiation.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0024" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>24</sup>
</xref> GATA2 deficiency is lethal due to the loss of hematopoietic stem cells in early embryogenesis,<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0025" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>25</sup>
</xref> with mutations that reduce GATA2 activity leading to the development of conditions such as MonoMac syndrome&#x02014;characterized by monocytopenia, NK (natural killer)&#x02010; and B&#x02010;lymphocytopenia, and a susceptibility to the development of myelodysplasia and acute myeloid leukemia.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0026" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>26</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0027" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>27</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0028" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>28</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>GATA2 expression oscillates during cell cycle, with this cyclical expression pattern required for the proliferation of hematopoietic stem cells.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0029" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>29</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0030" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>30</sup>
</xref> During G1 phase, GATA2 expression is suppressed by CDK4.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref> Loss of this suppression increases GATA2 expression, where via inducing the expression of MYB, GATA2 mediates the G1/S transition.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref> Once in S&#x02010;phase, GATA2 induces expression of cyclin D1 and cyclin E1, which initiates a positive feedback loop that sustains GATA2 expression through G2 phase.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref> The high levels of these cyclins and GATA2 then mediates passage through the G2/M checkpoint, thereby completing the proliferative cycle. Given the key role of GATA2 in the cell cycle, and its expression in plaque macrophages, we used human atheroma samples and human macrophage cell lines to test the hypothesis that GATA2 expression mediates the proliferation of plaque macrophages.</p><sec sec-type="methods" id="jah311546-sec-0009"><title>METHODS</title><sec sec-type="data-availability" id="jah311546-sec-0010"><title>Data Availability</title><p>The microarray data were published previously and are available as Borealis data set WYSAA0, <ext-link xlink:href="https://doi.org/10.5683/SP2/WYSAA0" ext-link-type="uri">https://doi.org/10.5683/SP2/WYSAA0</ext-link>, and at Gene Expression Omnibus (accession GSE86984). Numerical data are available in the article and in its online supplementary materials. Raw microscopy images will be shared on reasonable request to the corresponding author.</p></sec><sec sec-type="materials" id="jah311546-sec-0011"><title>Materials</title><p>pGL4.20[luc2/Puro] was a gift from Dr Rodney DeKoter, University of Western Ontario. Lympholyte poly and THP1 human monocytic cells (ATCC TIB 202) were purchased from Cedarlane Labs (Burlington, Canada). THP1 cells transduced with a GATA2 overexpression cassette, or GATA2&#x02010;targeting shRNA, were generated previously.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> Information on all antibodies used in this study can be found in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1</xref>. Information on all primers used in this study can be found in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S2</xref>; all primers and synthetic DNA blocks were from IDT (Coralville IA). Human TruStain Fc Block and the CD14 cell Mojosort enrichment kit were from Biolegend (San Diego, CA). FlowJo software, blood collection sets, and heparinized vacuum tubes were purchased from Becton&#x02010;Dickinson (Franklin Lakes, NJ). Aortic punches were purchased from QUEST Medical (London, Canada). OCT freezing compound and tissue cassettes were from Sakura Finetek (Torrance, CA). DMEM, RPMI, 100&#x000d7; antibiotic/antimycotic, and fetal bovine serum (FBS) were purchased from Wisent (Saint&#x02010;Jean&#x02010;Baptiste, Canada). Recombinant human M&#x02010;CSF, PDGF (platelet&#x02010;derived growth factor)&#x02010;AA/AB, TNF&#x003b1; (tumor necrosis factor alpha), and IL&#x02010;23 (interleukin&#x02010;23) were from Peprotech (Rocky Hill, NJ). Cholesterol and triglyceride quantification kits and Oil&#x02010;Red&#x02010;O were from Abcam (Cambridge, UK), and 96&#x02010;well luciferase plates were from Greiner Bio&#x02010;One (Kremsm&#x000fc;nster, Austria). Coverslips, 16% paraformaldehyde, and immersion oil were from Electron Microscopy Sciences (Hatfield, PA). Human Coronary Artery Endothelial Cells and endothelial cell culture media were from Promocell (Heidelberg, Germany). Ibidi &#x003bc;&#x02010;Slide VI<sup>0.4</sup> flow chambers were from Ibidi GbmH (Gr&#x000e4;felfing, Germany). HiFI Gibson assembly mix, restriction enzymes, T4 DNA ligase, and Q4 polymerase chain reaction (PCR) mastermix were purchased from NEB Canada (Whitby, Canada). Amaxa nucleofector kits were purchased from Lonza Canada (Kingston, Canada). Luciferase detection kits were from Promega (Madison WI). Accell cell&#x02010;penetrant siRNAs and Accell medium was from Horizon Discovery (Waterbeach, UK). RNA isolation kits were from Qiagen (Hilden, Germany) or GeneBio Systems (Burlington, Canada). iScript cDNA kits and SsoFast Evagreen Supermix were from Biorad (Hercules, CA). Prism software was from Graphpad Software (Boston, MA). Common laboratory chemicals were purchased from Bioshop Canada (Mississauga, Canada). All other materials were purchased from ThermoFisher (Waltham, MA).</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0012"><title>Ethics and Sample Collection</title><p>This study used blood and tissue samples from patients undergoing CABG surgery at University Hospital, London, Ontario, Canada. The study was reviewed and approved by the Human Research Ethics Committee of the Western University Health Sciences Research Ethics Board (approval numbers: 107566 and 104&#x02009;010) and all procedures followed were in accordance with institutional guidelines. All participants gave informed consent before their inclusion in the study, and this study conformed to the principles outlined in the Declaration of Helsinki. To be included, CABG patients had to be over the age of 18 and have a positive diagnosis of myocardial infract. To limit the impact of acute inflammatory responses and immunosuppression, patients undergoing CABG were excluded from enrollment in the study if they had a myocardial infarct within the past month, were diagnosed with an immunodeficiency disorder, were taking immunosuppressive medications, or had blood cancer or any other disease affecting their bone marrow. Routine lipid markers, patient age, and patient sex were recorded at the time of surgery but were not used as inclusion or exclusion criteria (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S3</xref>). Blood was collected from patients into heparinized tubes during preoperative procedures. Aortic punch tissue was obtained intraoperatively using a 4.0&#x02009;mm diameter CleanCut RCL Aortic Punch and immediately placed into cold saline. This discarded aorta tissue was then halved, with one half stored at &#x02212;80&#x02009;&#x000b0;C after flash freezing in liquid nitrogen, and the other embedded in OCT freezing compound for sectioning. Although these patients have atherosclerosis sever enough to require CABG surgery, these biopsies are taken from visually normal regions to ensure firm attachment of the grafted blood vessel. As such, plaques in these biopsies are typically limited to small, minimally developed lesions. To be included, healthy controls had to have an age&#x02009;&#x02265;&#x02009;the age of the youngest patient undergoing CABG (55.8&#x02009;years), have no preexisting diagnosis of atherosclerosis or hyperlipidemia, have taken no prescription medications in the past 4&#x02009;weeks, and self&#x02010;reported as being healthy on the day of donation. Blood from these donors was collected by venipuncture into heparinized tubes. Frozen sections of pathologist&#x02010;scored advanced cardiac vessel plaque, and skin, liver, and adipose tissue from the same patient, were purchased from Origene (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S3</xref>).</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0013"><title>Peripheral Blood Monocyte and Plasma Isolation</title><p>Peripheral blood mononuclear cells were isolated from blood using density gradient centrifugation. Initially, blood was centrifuged at 400<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> for 10&#x02009;minutes to separate plasma, and the pellet diluted with a volume of PBS equal to the starting volume of blood. Then, 3 to 4&#x02009;mL of lympholyte poly were placed into a 15&#x02009;mL tube and the diluted blood layered on top. This tube was centrifuged at 500<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> for 25&#x02009;minutes with medium acceleration and no brake. The top band of mononuclear cells was carefully collected into a 50&#x02009;mL tube and washed with 50&#x02009;mL PBS (137&#x02009;mmol/L NaCl, 10&#x02009;mmol/L Na<sub>2</sub>HPO<sub>4</sub>, pH 7.4), then centrifuged at 400<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> for 5&#x02009;minutes with maximum acceleration and braking. After washing, cells were suspended in 10&#x02009;mL PBS and counted. In some experiments, monocytes were purified from this mixed population by positive&#x02010;selection magnetic sorting using a Mojosort kit as per the manufacturer&#x02019;s instructions. Cells were then washed again at 400<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> for 5&#x02009;minutes and suspended in either culture medium (RPMI +10% FBS+1:100 antimycotic/antibiotic) for immediate use or were frozen in freezing medium (90% FBS+10% dimethyl sulfoxide) for later experiments.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0014"><title>Differentiation of Monocyte&#x02010;Derived Macrophages</title><p>Macrophages were differentiated from peripheral blood mononuclear cells using either cytokines or autologous plasma; 2.5&#x000d7;10<sup>5</sup> peripheral blood mononuclear cells in culture medium were placed into each well of a 12&#x02010;well tissue culture plate and incubated in a 5% CO<sub>2</sub>/37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C incubator for 1&#x02009;hour, then nonadherent cells removed with 2 gentle washes with prewarmed PBS. For cytokine differentiation, the final PBS wash was replaced with culture medium +10&#x02009;ng/mL human M&#x02010;CSF. The cells were incubated for 3&#x02009;days in a 5% CO<sub>2</sub>/37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C incubator, the medium replaced with fresh culture medium +10&#x02009;ng/mL human M&#x02010;CSF, and 3&#x02009;days later the cells were used for experiments. For autologous plasma differentiation, the final PBS wash was replaced with culture medium +10% autologous plasma. The cells were incubated for 3&#x02009;days in a 5% CO<sub>2</sub>/37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C incubator, then the medium replaced with fresh culture medium +10% autologous plasma, and 3&#x02009;days later the cells were used for experiments.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0015"><title>Transcriptomics Analysis</title><p>Transcriptomes of GATA2+ macrophages and monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages (moMacs) derived from age&#x02010; and sex&#x02010;matched controls were published previously (Borealis data set WYSAA0, <ext-link xlink:href="https://doi.org/10.5683/SP2/WYSAA0" ext-link-type="uri">https://doi.org/10.5683/SP2/WYSAA0</ext-link>).<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> In these data sets GATA2+ macrophages were isolated from early&#x02010;stage atheromas by staining 10&#x02009;&#x003bc;m thick section of aortic punch biopsies for GATA2 and the pan&#x02010;human&#x02010;macrophage marker CD163,<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0033" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>33</sup>
</xref> followed by laser microdissection using a ArcturusXT Laser Capture Microdissection System (Applied Biosystems) using CapSure HS LCM Caps.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> Healthy donor monocyte transcriptomes acquired using the same microarray platform were from Farnia et&#x000a0;al., Gene Expression Omnibus accession GSE86984.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0034" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>34</sup>
</xref> All data sets used the Affymetrix Human Gene 2.0 ST Array and analysis was performed in Bioconductor.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0035" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>35</sup>
</xref> First, raw microarray data were imported, converted to expression values, and quality confirmed via the arrayQualityMetrics command. Robust multichip average was then calculated for all transcriptsw and differentially expressed genes identified as those with &#x0003e;2&#x02010;fold change in expression between any 2 groups. Principal component analysis was performed on the differentially expressed genes, and a gene expression heatmap was prepared by calculating the Manhattan distance between all differentially expressed genes that was then plotted using the pheatmap command. The genes from individual gene clusters were extracted and their enrichment into reactomes calculated using ReactomePA. Cytoscape was used to identify transcriptional networks by first identifying proteins in the GATA2+ macrophage transcriptome that are identified as GATA2 interacting proteins in the IMEx data set. Once these first&#x02010;order interactions were identified, the network was expanded to include second&#x02010; and third&#x02010;order interactions, and the resulting protein interaction network plotted in Cyotscape.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0036" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>36</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0016"><title>Chromatin Immunoprecipitation<sc>&#x02010;</sc>
<styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">PCR</styled-content>
</title><p>Chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP)&#x02010;PCR was performed using a Megna&#x02010;ChIP kit as per the manufacturer&#x02019;s instructions. Briefly, 2.0&#x000d7;10<sup>7</sup> GATA2&#x02010;overexpressing THP&#x02010;1 macrophages were differentiated in a 150&#x02009;mm culture dish, washed thrice with PBS, fixed (10&#x02009;minutes, 1% formaldehyde) and quenched using 1% glycine. The cells were scrapped into cold PBS with HALT protease inhibitors, centrifuged (5&#x02009;minutes, 300<italic toggle="yes">g</italic>), resuspended in lysis buffer, then incubated on ice for 15&#x02009;minutes with occasional agitation. Nuclei were collected, suspended in nuclear lysis buffer, and DNA sheared by sonicated with a Bioruptor (Diagenode, Denvile NJ), using 15 cycles of a 30&#x02009;seconds on/30&#x02009;seconds off to generate 300&#x02009;bp fragments. The samples were then centrifuged to remove insoluble materials. Rabbit antihuman GATA2 antibody (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1</xref>) and 20&#x02009;&#x003bc;L of protein A/G magnetic beads were added 50&#x02009;&#x003bc;g of sheared DNA and incubated overnight at 4&#x02009;&#x000b0;C on a rotating shaker. Protein A/G beads were separated using a magnetic separator and the supernatant removed. The protein A/G beads were then sequentially washed with low salt wash buffer, high salt wash buffer, LiCl wash buffer, and TE buffer, using the magnetic separator after each wash to remove the beads. Elution buffer with proteinase K was then added and incubated at 62&#x02009;&#x000b0;C for 2&#x02009;hours, then incubated at 95&#x02009;&#x000b0;C for 10&#x02009;minutes. The beads were removed via magnetic separation, and the DNA purified form the supernatant using the Megna&#x02010;ChIP kit. 5&#x02009;&#x003bc;L of the resulting DNA suspension was used for PCR, using a Taq PCR kit and the primers indicated in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S2</xref>, using 35 cycles of 98&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/30&#x02009;seconds, 55&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/30&#x02009;seconds, and 72&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/60&#x02009;seconds to amplify the DNA. Amplified DNA (~150&#x02009;bp) was resolved on a 2% agarose gel.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0017"><title>Flow Cytometry</title><p>Sorted CD14+ cells or whole peripheral blood mononuclear cells were first blocked with Human TruStain FcX blocker for 10&#x02009;minutes at room temperature. After washing, cells were stained with human anti&#x02010;CD14&#x02010;APC antibody and fixable viability dye&#x02010;eFluor780 for 45&#x02009;minutes at 4&#x02009;&#x000b0;C. For intranuclear GATA2 staining, the cells were fixed and permeabilized with flow cytometry permeabilization solution (ThermoFisher), followed by addition of human anti&#x02010;GATA2&#x02010;AF555 antibody for 1&#x02009;hour at room temperature. After washing, the cells were resuspended in FACS buffer (2% FBS in PBS) and a minimum of 10&#x02009;000 cells acquired on a BD FACSCanto II. FlowJo was used to analyze the resulting data by first gating on forward and side scatter, selecting singlets with the forward scatter height/&#x02212;area channels, live cells gated via the eF780 channel, and monocytes identified as CD14+ cells in the gated population. The mean fluorescence intensity for GATA2 was then determined for the CD14+ cell population. The specific antibodies and staining conditions used for flow cytometry are listed in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1</xref>.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0018"><title>Luminex Cytokine Assays</title><p>Plasma samples were platelet&#x02010;depleted by centrifuging at 21000<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> for 30&#x02009;seconds. Then, 100&#x02009;&#x003bc;L of the clarified plasma was frozen and sent to Eve Technologies Corporation (Calgary, Canada) for analysis on a Luminex 200 system and Eve Technologies Human Cytokine 96&#x02010;Plex Discovery Assay. Quantified cytokines included the following: sCD40L, EGF, Eotaxin, FGF&#x02010;2, FLT&#x02010;3 Ligand, Fractalkine, G&#x02010;CSF, GM&#x02010;CSF, GRO&#x003b1;, IFN&#x02010;&#x003b1;2, IFN&#x02010;&#x003b3;, IL&#x02010;1&#x003b1;, IL&#x02010;1&#x003b2;, IL&#x02010;1RA, IL&#x02010;2, IL&#x02010;3, IL&#x02010;4, IL&#x02010;5, IL&#x02010;6, IL&#x02010;7, IL&#x02010;8, IL&#x02010;9, IL&#x02010;10, IL&#x02010;12(p40), IL&#x02010;12(p70), IL&#x02010;13, IL&#x02010;15, IL&#x02010;17A, IL&#x02010;17E/IL&#x02010;25, IL&#x02010;17F, IL&#x02010;18, IL&#x02010;22, IL&#x02010;27, IP&#x02010;10, MCP&#x02010;1, MCP&#x02010;3, M&#x02010;CSF, MDC, MIG/CXCL9, MIP&#x02010;1&#x003b1;, MIP&#x02010;1&#x003b2;, PDGF&#x02010;AA, PDGF&#x02010;AB/BB, RANTES, TGF&#x003b1;, TNF&#x02010;&#x003b1;, TNF&#x02010;&#x003b2;, VEGF&#x02010;A, 6Ckine, APRIL, BAFF, BCA&#x02010;1, CCL28, CTACK, CXCL16, ENA&#x02010;78, Eotaxin&#x02010;2, Eotaxin&#x02010;3, GCP&#x02010;2, Granzyme A, Granzyme B, HMGB1, I&#x02010;309, I&#x02010;TAC, IFN&#x003b2;, IFN&#x003c9;, IL&#x02010;11, IL&#x02010;16, IL&#x02010;20, IL&#x02010;21, IL&#x02010;23, IL&#x02010;24, IL&#x02010;28A, IL&#x02010;29, IL&#x02010;31, IL&#x02010;33, IL&#x02010;34, IL&#x02010;35, LIF, Lymphotactin, MCP&#x02010;2, MCP&#x02010;4, MIP&#x02010;1&#x003b4;, MIP&#x02010;3&#x003b1;, MIP&#x02010;3&#x003b2;, MPIF&#x02010;1, Perforin, sCD137, SCF, SDF&#x02010;1, sFAS, sFASL, TARC, TPO, TRAIL, and TSLP.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0019"><title>Cholesterol and Triglyceride Quantification</title><p>Total cholesterol, HDL, and LDL cholesterol were measured in the patient&#x02019;s plasma using a cholesterol assay kit. For total cholesterol, whole plasma was used, whereas HDL and LDL were quantified using a 1:1 mixture of plasma and 2&#x000d7; Precipitation Buffer. Plasma was centrifuged for 10&#x02009;minutes at 2000&#x02009;&#x000d7;&#x02009;<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> and the supernatant used for HDL&#x02010;cholesterol measurement, and the precipitate was resuspended in 100&#x02009;&#x003bc;L PBS for measuring the LDL/very LDL fraction. Next, 50&#x02009;&#x003bc;L volumes of reaction mix were added to the total cholesterol, HDL&#x02010;cholesterol, LDL&#x02010;cholesterol, and standard curve samples according to the manufacturer protocol. The mixture was incubated for 60&#x02009;minutes at 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C and read on a microplate reader with excitation at 535&#x02009;nm and emission at 587&#x02009;nm. Triglycerides were measured in patient&#x02019;s plasma using a triglyceride assay kit. Then, 2&#x02009;&#x003bc;L of cholesterol esterase/lipase was added to 10&#x02009;&#x003bc;L aliquots of plasma and incubated for 20&#x02009;minutes at RT with constant agitation. Next, triglyceride assay buffer, OxiRed probe, and triglyceride enzyme mix were added to a final volume of 50&#x02009;&#x003bc;L in 96&#x02010;well plates and incubated for 60&#x02009;minutes at RT. The plate was read on a microplate reader with an excitation of 535&#x02009;nm and an emission of 587&#x02009;nm.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0020"><title>Parallel Plate Flow Chamber</title><p>A parallel plate flow chamber was used to simulate aortic monocyte recruitment on primary human aortic endothelial cells. Human coronary artery endothelial cells from an 84&#x02010;year&#x02010;old male patient were seeded in endothelial cell growth medium into an Ibidi &#x003bc;&#x02010;Slide VI<sup>0.4</sup> flow chamber. Cells were cultured at 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/5% CO<sub>2</sub> until confluent, typically 48 to 72&#x02009;hours. To mimic plaque inflammation, the media was replaced with medium containing 5&#x02009;ng/mL human TNF&#x003b1;, followed by incubation for 4&#x02009;hours at 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/5% CO<sub>2</sub>. The &#x003bc;&#x02010;Slide VI<sup>0.4</sup> was placed on the heated stage of a Leica DMI600B microscope equipped with an Inscoper control unit (Inscoper, Cesson&#x02010;S&#x000e9;vign&#x000e9;, France), and imaged with DIC illumination using a 40&#x000d7;/1.30 NA oil immersion lens. During imaging, sorted CD14+ cells were perfused through the flow chamber with an Orion Sage M362 syringe pump at a rate of 39.5&#x02009;mL/min, producing a surface shear force of 50&#x02009;dynes/cm<sup>2</sup>. Timelapses of monocyte recruitment were captured for 30&#x02009;minutes, imaging every 30&#x02009;seconds. The resulting Images were analyzed in FIJI to quantify monocyte rolling, adhesion, and transmigration across the endothelium as described previously.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0037" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>37</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0038" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>38</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0021"><title>Tissue Imaging</title><p>For aortic biopsies, OCT embedded tissues were sliced into 8&#x02009;&#x003bc;m thick sections using a Leica CM18602 cryostat, and slices placed onto glass slides. For advanced atheromas, presliced 10&#x02009;&#x003bc;m thick sections of pathologist&#x02010;scored human coronary arteries from patients with atherosclerosis were purchased from Origene, selecting patients with a range of disease severity. Slides were kept frozen at &#x02212;80&#x02009;&#x000b0;C until processed. For Oil&#x02010;Red&#x02010;O staining, the tissue was fixed in ice&#x02010;cold 10% formalin for 10&#x02009;minutes, rinsed in 3 changes of distilled water, air dried, then immersed in propylene glycol for 5&#x02009;minutes. The tissue was then stained in prewarmed 0.5% Oil Red O solution in propylene glycol for 10&#x02009;minutes in a 60&#x02009;&#x000b0;C oven. Following staining, the tissue was destained in 85% propylene glycol in water for 5&#x02009;minutes and rinsed with 2 changes of distilled water. For counterstaining, the tissue was treated with Mayer&#x02019;s hematoxylin for 30&#x02009;seconds, washed under running water for 3&#x02009;minutes, and placed in distilled water for an additional 3&#x02009;minutes. The stained tissue was then mounted on a coverslip using an aqueous mounting medium and imaged. For fluorescence staining, samples were thawed, fixed in fresh 4% paraformaldehyde in PIPES buffer (0.1&#x02009;mmol/L PIPES, 5&#x02009;mmol/L EGTA, 2&#x02009;mmol/L MgCl2 6H<sub>2</sub>O) for 10&#x02009;minutes at 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C, then washed with PBS 3 times in Coplin Jars. Samples were blocked with PBS&#x02009;+&#x02009;10% Normal Donkey Serum +0.1% Triton X&#x02010;100 +0.01% NaN<sub>3</sub> at for 1&#x02009;hour. Samples were stained with primary anti&#x02010;CD68 and anti&#x02010;Ki67 antibodies for 1.5&#x02009;hours at 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C. After 3 washes in fresh PBS, the samples were stained with 0.67&#x02009;&#x003bc;g/mL of antirabbit 488 and antimouse 647 secondary antibodies for 45&#x02009;minutes. The samples were washed 3 times with PBS and stained with anti&#x02010;GATA2&#x02010;AF555 for 1&#x02009;hour. To stain nuclei, 1&#x02009;&#x003bc;g/mL of Hoechst in PBS was added and incubated for 10&#x02009;minutes. Following a final PBS wash, a coverslip was mounted on top of the samples with an aqueous mounting medium. Samples were imaged using a 20&#x000d7;/0.75 NA objective on a Leica DMI 5500B microscope equipped with a halogen light source, filters for DAPI, FITC, Cy3, and Cy5 fluorophores, operated using LAS&#x02010;AS software. Histological samples were imaged using a color camera and brightfield illumination, whereas fluorescence samples were imaged using the appropriate filter sets. Fluorescent samples were then 2&#x02010;dimensionally deconvolved using a 30&#x02010;iteration Richardson&#x02010;Lucy algorithm in DeconvolutionLab2.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0039" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>39</sup>
</xref> To perform a quantitative analysis of macrophage phenotypes, blinded images were used to train the QuPath machine learning algorithm.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0040" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>40</sup>
</xref> As Ki&#x02010;67, GATA2, and CD68 exhibit punctate staining in cells, QuPath was first trained to identify puncta of the expected size and shape for each marker and to ignore the collagen autofluorescence in each channel, which appears as long, linear signals. Next, nuclei were segmented from the DAPI channel, and each nucleus scored for the presence of Ki&#x02010;67 and GATA2. Finally, the regions of interest around each nucleus were expanded to detect the presence of the macrophage lysosomal marker CD68, and the detections and classifications exported for analysis. To analyze the positioning of cells within these lesions, the lesion was first identified in the Oil&#x02010;Red&#x02010;O and hematoxylin&#x02010;stained section. A region of interest was drawn around the corresponding area in the fluorescent image analyzed using QuPath. The region of interest, along with the <italic toggle="yes">x</italic>/<italic toggle="yes">y</italic> coordinates and cell phenotypes, was exported to Matlab. Two vectors were assigned to the image: (1) the lesional axis, which ran parallel to the vessel lumen along the length of the lesion, and (2) the lesional thickness, which was perpendicular to the lesional axis and extended from the intimal to the luminal side of the lesion. The Euclidean distance from each cell to the nearest edge of the region of interest (ie, the edge of the lesion) was calculated. This distance was then normalized to the lesion&#x02019;s width at the respective position along the lesional axis.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0022"><title>
<styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">THP1</styled-content> Cell Culture</title><p>Wild&#x02010;type THP1 human monocytes, and THP1 cells ectopically overexpressing GATA2, or expressing a GATA2&#x02010;targeting shRNA, were maintained as suspension cultures in RPMI +10% FBS in a 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/5% CO<sub>2</sub> incubator. Cells were grown to a density of 1&#x000d7;10<sup>6</sup>/mL and then split by diluting 1:5 into fresh medium. To differentiate the cells into macrophages, 2.5&#x000d7;10<sup>5</sup> cells were placed into each well of a 12&#x02010;well plate, and the cells incubated in RPMI +10% FBS +100&#x02009;ng/mL phorbol 12&#x02010;myristate 13&#x02010;acetate for 72&#x02009;hours. For experiments involving microscopy, #1.5 thickness, 18&#x02009;mm diameter circular coverslips were cleaned by acid washing (2 M HCl in ddH<sub>2</sub>O, 56&#x02009;&#x000b0;C, overnight), autoclaved, and then placed into the wells of the 12&#x02010;well plate before plating cells.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0023"><title>Luciferase Assays</title><p>The MYB and JUN promoters were ordered as synthetic DNA constructs. The promoters were inserted into pGL4.20[luc2/Puro] vector via Gibson assembly by first linearizing the vector with an EcoRV digest, followed by gel purification, and assembly of the final vector using a HiFi Gibson Assembly Kit. Successful cloning was confirmed by Sanger sequencing at the London Genomics Centre (London, Canada). Wild type THP&#x02010;1 or GATA2&#x02010;overexpressed THP&#x02010;1 cells were transfected with 0.5 &#x003bc;g of DNA using an Amaxa Human Monocyte Nucleofector kit and a Lonza Nucleofector II, using the V&#x02010;001 protocol. After transfection, 500 &#x003bc;L of warm RPMI +10% FBS was added to the electroporation cuvette. Transfected cells were then transferred to a 12&#x02010;well plate and incubated in a humidified 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/5% CO<sub>2</sub> incubator overnight. The next day, macrophage differentiation was induced, as described earlier. Following differentiation, the cells were lysed with 250 &#x003bc;L passive lysis buffer and rocking for 20 minutes at room temperature. Lysates were collected centrifuged at 21000<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> for 30&#x02009;seconds to remove debris, and 100&#x02009;&#x003bc;L of lysate added to each well of a 96&#x02010;well white polystyrene plate. Then, 50&#x02009;&#x003bc;L of luciferase assay reagent II was added to the well, and the plate immediately read using 2(L) Lum, linear shaking, using a 10&#x02009;seconds acquisition in Cytation Gen5 software. Duplicate wells for all measurements were used, with each biological replicate being the average of these duplicate technical repeats.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0024"><title>Proliferation Assays</title><p>To quantify proliferation during macrophage differentiation, THP&#x02010;1 monocytes were prestained with Cell Proliferation Dye eFluor 670 by suspending the cells in PBS +5&#x02009;&#x003bc;mol/L Cell Proliferation eFluor 670 dye for 5&#x02009;minutes, followed by pelleting the cells with a 400<italic toggle="yes">g</italic> centrifugation and resuspending the cells in RPMI +10% FBS +100&#x02009;ng/mL phorbol 12&#x02010;myristate 13&#x02010;acetate. For quantification of macrophage proliferation, THP1 macrophages were differentiated as described earlier, treated for 24 hours with 1 &#x003bc;M of either a nontargeting (scrambled) or MYB&#x02010;targeting Accell cell&#x02010;penetrant siRNAs in Accell medium, cultured for an additional 24 hours in RPMI +10% FBS, then stimulated with 2.5&#x02009;mmol/L (~100&#x02009;&#x003bc;g/mL) oxLDL. For quantification of proliferation during foam cell formation, THP&#x02010;1 monocytes were differentiated into macrophages as described and then labeled with 5 &#x003bc;M Cell Proliferation eFluor 670 dye for 5&#x02009;minutes, followed by a wash with PBS and addition of RPMI +10% FBS+ 2.5&#x02009;mmol/L (~100&#x02009;&#x003bc;g/mL) oxLDL. As a control, cells are fixed with paraformaldehyde immediately after labeling with Cell Proliferation Dye. Immediately after phorbol 12&#x02010;myristate 13&#x02010;acetate or oxLDL stimulation an 11&#x000d7;11 tiled region was imaged in DIC and far&#x02010;red fluorescent channels, and the samples returned to a 37&#x02009;&#x000b0;C/5% CO<sub>2</sub> incubator, with the samples reimaged at 4 and 24&#x02009;hours. All imaging was performed using identical excitation and camera settings to enable fluorescence quantification between time points. Imaging was performed on a Leca DMI6000B equipped with a Photometrics Evolve 512 EM&#x02010;CCD camera, Sola light engine, Chroma Sedat Quad filter set with blue (Ex: 380/30, Em: 455/50), green (Ex: 490/20, Em: 525/36), red (Ex: 555/25, Em: 605/52), and far&#x02010;red (Ex: 645/30, Em: 705/72) filter pairs, and the LAS&#x02010;X software platform, using a 40&#x000d7;/1.30 NA objective lens. The resulting tiles were assembled into a single large image using LAS&#x02010;X software, and individual macrophages segmented using the far&#x02010;red channel and a trained machine learning algorithm in Ilastik.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0041" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>41</sup>
</xref> The resulting cell masks were then imported into FIJI, and the integrated intensity of the Cell Proliferation Dye eFluor 670 in each cell quantified using the &#x0201c;Analyze Particles&#x0201d; tool.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0038" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>38</sup>
</xref> Data are presented as either the fraction of cells that underwent proliferation (eg, intensity of the Cell Proliferation eFluor 670 decreased by &#x0003e;50%) or as the integrated intensity of individual cells.</p><p>For differentiation dynamics, wild&#x02010;type and GATA2&#x02010;overepxressing macrophages were placed into the wells of a customized live&#x02010;cell imaging chamber,<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0042" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>42</sup>
</xref> stimulated with 100&#x02009;ng/mL phorbol 12&#x02010;myristate 13&#x02010;acetate, and placed onto the heated and CO<sub>2</sub>&#x02010;perfused stage of our Leca DMI6000B microscope. DIC images were captured every 30&#x02009;minutes for 72&#x02009;hours. These time lapses were imported into Ilastik, where individual cells were segmented with a custom&#x02010;trained segmentation algorithm. The segmented images were imported into FIJI, and using the particle analyzer tool, the area and perimeter of each cell determined. The differentiation of the macrophages, defined as their change in shape from spherical monocytes to a macrophage shape, was quantified by calculation of the form factor (perimeter<sup>2</sup>/(4&#x003c0;*Area)).</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0025"><title>Reverse Transcription&#x02010;PCR</title><p>Total RNA was isolated from monocytes, monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages (moMacs), and THP1 cells using an RNA isolation kit and converted to cDNA using an iScript cDNA kit. Quantitative PCR was run for samples using SsoFast Evagreen Supermix on a Quantico Quantitative PCR thermocycler. Gene expression was calculated using the &#x00394;&#x00394;Ct method, using GAPDH as a reference gene. All primer sequences used in quantitative&#x02010;PCR are shown in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S2</xref>.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0026"><title>Apoptosis Assays</title><p>THP1 macrophages were differentiated on glass coverslips as described and then stimulated for 24&#x02009;hours with either 10&#x02009;mmol/L oxLDL or 10&#x02009;&#x003bc;mol/L thapsigargin for 48&#x02009;hours. The cells were then cooled to room temperature and stained with Alexa&#x02010;488 conjugated AnnexinV in binding buffer (150&#x02009;mmol/L NaCl, 2&#x02009;mmol/L CaCl<sub>2</sub>, 20&#x02009;mmol/L HEPES, pH 7.4) for 10&#x02009;minutes. The cells were washed once with binding buffer, fixed with 4% paraformaldehyde in binding buffer, counterstained with 1&#x02009;&#x003bc;g/mL DAPI for 5&#x02009;minutes, washed once with PBS, and then mounted on a glass slide using Permount Fluorescent Mounting Medium. An 11&#x000d7;11 tiled area of each sample was imaged as described in the proliferation assay, the resulting tiles were assembled into a single large image using LAS&#x02010;X software, and the portion of cells positive for AnnexinV staining determined using the Cell Counting feature in FIJI.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0038" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>38</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0027"><title>Statistical Analysis</title><p>Statistics were performed in Graphpad Prism version 9. Data are presented as individual measurements plotted over the mean&#x000b1;SEM and tested for normality using a Shapiro&#x02013;Wilk test before analysis. Unless otherwise noted, all statistics were performed using nonparametric 2&#x02010;tailed test Kruskal&#x02013;Wallis test with Dunn correction or a Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="jah311546-sec-0028"><title>RESULTS</title><sec id="jah311546-sec-0228"><title>Characterization of GATA2+ Cells in Early Atheromas</title><p>We previously determined that a large portion of macrophages present in early&#x02010;stage aortic atheromas express GATA2, but the ontology and role of these GATA2+ macrophages in atherosclerosis remain unclear.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> Using aortic biopsies collected during CABG that contain small, early&#x02010;stage plaque (Stary scores of 1 or 2), we quantified the number of GATA2+ cells present in these tissues (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1A</xref> and <xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1B</xref>). As expected of early&#x02010;stage lesions, no extracellular lipid accumulation was observed in these sections, with lipid accumulation restricted to cell&#x02010;size inclusions in the intima (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1A</xref>, Oil Red O staining), with macrophage infiltration observed in the same regions as the lipid inclusions (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1A</xref>, CD68 staining). The portion of cells expressing GATA2 was then quantified in these early&#x02010;stage lesions. Although the portion of total cells that expressed GATA2 varied greatly between patients, &#x0003e;90% of GATA2 expressing cells in each lesion were CD68+ macrophages (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1B</xref>). In later stages of disease smooth muscle cells can take on a macrophage&#x02010;like phenotype, including the expression of CD68.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0043" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>43</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0044" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>44</sup>
</xref> To determine whether the GATA2+ cells were macrophages or CD68&#x02010;expressing smooth muscle cells, we reanalyzed our previous microarray analysis of aortic punch biopsy macrophages for markers of smooth muscle cells (Tagln [transgelin], Acta2 [alpha&#x02010;smooth muscle actin], and CNN1 [calponin 1]) and macrophages (ITGAM [integrin alpha M], CD163, and CD68), identifying a clear macrophage phenotype (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1C</xref>).<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> The portion of intimal macrophages that expressed GATA2 increased with macrophage density within the early lesions, suggesting that either GATA2+ macrophages were being recruited to these lesions or that the local environment was inducing GATA2 expression in these cells (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1A</xref>). Combined, these results confirm our previous observations that GATA2+ macrophages infiltrate early&#x02010;stage atherosclerotic lesions and demonstrate that the expression of GATA2 correlates with the degree of macrophage infiltration into these lesions.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="Figure" id="jah311546-fig-0001"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><title>GATA2+ macrophages exhibit a transcriptional profile intermediary to monocytes and monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages.</title><p>
<bold>A</bold>, Micrographs of serial sections of an aortic punch biopsy, stained for lipids (Oil&#x02010;Red&#x02010;O) and cells (hematoxylin), or fluorescently stained for DNA (DAPI, cyan), GATA2 (magenta), and macrophages (CD68, yellow). Insert shows an early&#x02010;stage plaque identified as a region containing foci of Oil&#x02010;Red&#x02010;O staining and increased cellularity. Oil&#x02010;Red&#x02010;O inclusions are indicated with white arrows. Cells were classified (Class) using QuPath based on their expression of CD68 and GATA2 and classified as macrophages (CD68+/GATA2&#x02212;, cyan), GATA2+ macrophages (CD68+/GATA2+, yellow), or as other (CD68&#x02212;/GATA2&#x02212;, red). No GATA2+ cells lacking CD68 were identified in this sample. Scale bar is 100&#x02009;&#x003bc;m. <bold>B</bold>, Percentage of macrophages (CD68+ cells, m&#x003c6;) vs nonmacrophages (CD68&#x02212; cells, Other) within early&#x02010;stage atherosclerotic lesions. <bold>C</bold>, Expression of smooth muscle cell, macrophage, and shared (Both) markers in GATA2+ macrophages recovered from aortic punch biopsies by CD163 laser microdissection. Numbers above each bar indicate the number of patient samples which had detectable levels of each transcript. <bold>D</bold>, Principal component analysis of the transcriptome of monocytes, GATA2+ macrophages (GATA2), and monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages. <bold>E</bold>, Transcriptome heatmap comparing monocytes, GATA2+ macrophages (GATA2), and monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages. Six clusters of genes under similar regulation were identified (numbered 1&#x02013;6), with cluster 2 (red) containing genes selectively upregulated in GATA2+ macrophages. <bold>F</bold>, GO analysis of Cluster 2. Count is the number of genes in cluster 2 assigned to the corresponding GO annotation. Adjusted <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values can be found in Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S4</xref>. <bold>G</bold> and <bold>H</bold>, Top differentially expressed genes in cluster 2 that are assigned to GO annotations for cell proliferation (<bold>F</bold>) and apoptosis (<bold>G</bold>). <bold>I</bold>, Expression of macrophage markers of embryonic (Runx1) vs hematopoietic (Kit, Tie2/TEK, and Flt3) ontology in monocytes, GATA2+ macrophages (GATA2), and monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages. <bold>J</bold>, reverse transcription polymerase chain reactionquantification of GATA2 expression in monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages differentiated from healthy controls (Controls) vs patients undergoing coronary artery bypass graft (Patients). GATA2 expression is normalized to GAPDH and expressed as fold&#x02010;expression relative to healthy controls. n=10 (<bold>B</bold>, <bold>C</bold>), 3 (<bold>D</bold> through <bold>I</bold>), or 6 (<bold>J</bold>) biological replicates, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values are shown between groups connected with a line, Kruskal&#x02013;Wallis with Dunn correction (<bold>F</bold> through <bold>H</bold>) or Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test (<bold>H</bold>). GO indicates Gene Ontology; IG; IS; n.d., not detected; oxLDL, oxidized low&#x02010;density lipoprotein; RACE; and TNF&#x003b1;, tumor necrosis factor alpha.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JAH3-14-e043646-g005" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></fig><p>To better understand the phenotype of these GATA2+ macrophages, we performed additional transcriptome analysis, comparing GATA2+ macrophages and in&#x000a0;vitro differentiated moMacs<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> to the transcriptome of healthy human monocytes.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0034" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>34</sup>
</xref> Analysis of these cells&#x02019; transcriptomes, followed by principal component analysis, determined that the gene expression profile of GATA2+ macrophages is intermediary to the transcriptional profiles of monocytes and moMacs (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1D</xref>), with &#x0003e;5100 genes differentially regulated between these cells (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1E</xref>). Consistent with the principal component analysis, GATA2+ macrophages exhibited partial downregulation of monocyte&#x02010;specific genes (Cluster 6, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1E</xref>) and partial upregulation of macrophage&#x02010;specific genes (Clusters 1, 3&#x02013;5, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1E</xref>). The GATA2+ macrophages also upregulated a subset of genes that were minimally expressed in monocytes and moMacs (Cluster 2, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1E</xref>). Gene ontology analysis of Cluster 2 identified an enrichment of genes involved in cell cycle and apoptosis (Figures&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1F</xref>, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1B</xref> through <xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S1E</xref>, Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S4</xref>). Consistent with this, several genes promoting cell proliferation and negatively regulating apoptosis were selectively upregulated in GATA2+ macrophages, including GATA2 itself (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1G</xref> and <xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1H</xref>). Monocytes, GATA2+ macrophages, and moMacs lacked expression of the yolk&#x02010;sac erythromyeloid progenitor marker Runx1, and expressed Kit, Tie2/TEK, and Flt3, consistent with an adult hematopoietic ontology (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1I</xref>).<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0045" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>45</sup>
</xref> However, macrophages expressed GATA2 only when they were differentiated from the monocytes of patients with atherosclerosis and not from healthy controls (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1J</xref>), suggesting that the upregulation of GATA2 during monocyte&#x02010;to&#x02010;macrophage differentiation is specific to atherogenic conditions and is not a normal part of monocyte&#x02010;to&#x02010;macrophage differentiation.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0328"><title>Monocytic GATA2 Expression Does Not Drive Macrophage GATA2 Expression</title><p>Given that GATA2+ macrophages were found in early&#x02010;stage atheromas of all tested patients undergoing CABG, where GATA2 expression may mediate monocyte&#x02010;to&#x02010;macrophage differentiation, we used flow cytometry to determine whether GATA2 was expressed in the monocytes purified from the same patients (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2A</xref>). GATA2 was minimally expressed in most healthy controls, with a portion of patients exhibiting higher levels of monocytic GATA2 expression (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2B</xref>). There was no statistically significant correlation between the expression level of GATA2 in patient monocytes and the frequency of GATA2+ macrophages in the patients&#x02019; atherosclerotic plaques was observed (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2C</xref>), nor was a significant correlation observed after removal of the outlier (R<sup>2</sup>=0.0624, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>=0.6853). Although there was no correlation between monocyte and macrophage GATA2 expression, the differing levels of GATA2 expression in patient monocytes may correlate with biomarkers of disease. To assess this possibility, we divided patients into GATA2&#x02010;high (GATA2 mean fluorescence intensity &#x0003e;490 A.U. above healthy controls) and GATA2&#x02010;low populations, then compared the expression of several monocyte&#x02010;associated markers that were expressed at high levels in GATA2+ macrophages between these populations (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1D</xref>). Although patients with atherosclerosis had an overall higher expression of the chemokine receptor CCR2 (C&#x02010;C chemokine receptor type 2) and of the &#x003b1;<sub>4</sub> and &#x003b2;<sub>2</sub> integrins than controls, there were no differences in the expression of these genes between GATA2&#x02010;high and GATA2&#x02010;low patient monocytes (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2D</xref> and <xref rid="jah311546-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2E</xref>). Consistent with these observations, no differences in the recruitment of GATA2&#x02010;high versus &#x02010;low patient monocytes to TNF&#x003b1;&#x02010;stimulated human aortic endothelium were observed in a parallel&#x02010;plate flow chamber assay of monocyte recruitment (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S2</xref>).</p><fig position="float" fig-type="Figure" id="jah311546-fig-0002"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><title>GATA2 is expressed in the monocytes of a portion of patients with atherosclerosis.</title><p>Flow cytometry was used to quantify the expression of GATA2 and other monocyte markers in peripheral blood mononuclear cells purified by positive selection from heathy controls and atherosclerosis patients undergoing coronary artery bypass graft surgery. <bold>A</bold>, Flow cytometry gating strategy for quantifying GATA2 expression in CD14+ monocytes. Plot is from a patient classified as having high monocytic GATA2 expression. <bold>B</bold>, Expression of GATA2 in monocytes from healthy controls and patients with atherosclerosis. <bold>C</bold>, There is no correlation between the MFI of GATA2 in patient monocytes vs the fraction of macrophages expressing GATA2 in early&#x02010;stage plaques from aortic punch biopsies taken during coronary artery bypass graft surgery. <bold>D</bold> and <bold>E</bold>, Expression of CCR2, &#x003b1;<sub>4</sub> integrin, and &#x003b2;<sub>2</sub> integrin in the monocytes of healthy control vs patients with atherosclerosis (<bold>D</bold>) and in patients with high (MFI &#x0003e;2 SD compared with healthy controls, &#x0201c;High&#x0201d;) vs low monocytic GATA2 expression (<bold>E</bold>). n=13 (<bold>B</bold>), 6 (<bold>C</bold> and <bold>D</bold>) or 3 (<bold>E</bold>) biological replicates, Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test (<bold>B</bold>, <bold>D</bold>, <bold>E</bold>), or Spearman&#x02019;s correlation coefficient (<bold>C</bold>). CCR2 indicates C&#x02010;C chemokine receptor type 2; Ctrl, Control; FSC&#x02010;A, forward scatter area; FSC&#x02010;H, forward scatter height; M&#x003c6;, macrophage; MFI, mean fluorescence intensity; Pt, Patient; and SSC&#x02010;A, side scatter area.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JAH3-14-e043646-g003" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig><p>To further explore potential links between monocyte GATA2 expression and biomarkers of atherosclerosis severity, we compared the cytokine, lipid, and sterol profiles of patients with GATA2&#x02010;low versus &#x02010;high monocytes. Luminex analysis of plasma cytokines identified minimal differences between controls and both GATA2&#x02010;low or GATA2&#x02010;high patients (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3A</xref>, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S3A</xref>). GATA2&#x02010;high patients exhibited an ~2&#x02010;fold increase in serum PDGF&#x02010;AB/BB and a &#x0003c;90% decrease in serum IL&#x02010;23 and IL&#x02010;17E (also known as IL&#x02010;25, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3B</xref> through <xref rid="jah311546-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3D</xref>). Neither PDGF&#x02010;A or PDGF&#x02010;B were upregulated in GATA2+ macrophages compared with monocytes or monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages, IL&#x02010;17E was not expressed at a measurable level in any of these cells, and opposite to what was observed in the circulation, IL&#x02010;23 expression was significantly elevated in GATA2+ macrophages (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3E</xref>). These data indicate that the circulating levels of these cytokines were not driven by GATA2+ macrophages and instead arise from other cell populations. GATA2 expression was modestly upregulated when monocytes were differentiated into moMacs in the presence of 12 ng/mL PDGF&#x02010;AB/BB, but this increase was not observed at other PDGF&#x02010;AB/BB concentrations (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3F</xref>). Patients with GATA2&#x02010;high monocytes had a modest increase in non&#x02010;HDL cholesterol but were otherwise similar to GATA2&#x02010;low patients across other lipid measures (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3G</xref>), and GATA2 expression was not induced in moMacs cultured with LDL (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3H</xref>). These results suggest that GATA2 expression arises due to the local plaque environment, rather than circulating cytokines or lipids.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="Figure" id="jah311546-fig-0003"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><title>Plasma cytokines and lipids do not drive GATA2 expression.</title><p>A 96&#x02010;plex Luminex panel was used to quantify cytokine concentrations in the serum of healthy controls vs atherosclerosis patients with high (MFI &#x0003e;2 SD compared with healthy controls, &#x0201c;High&#x0201d;) or low monocytic GATA2 expression. <bold>A</bold>, Heatmap of serum concentrations of all 96 cytokines in healthy controls vs patients with low or high monocytic GATA2 expression. <bold>B&#x02010;D</bold>, Serum concentration of PDGF&#x02010;AB/BB (<bold>B</bold>) and IL&#x02010;23 (<bold>C</bold>), and IL&#x02010;17E (I&#x02010;25) (<bold>D</bold>); the only cytokines significantly different in GATA2&#x02010;High patients vs healthy controls. <bold>E</bold>, Expression of PDGF&#x02010;A, PDGF&#x02010;B, IL&#x02010;23, and IL&#x02010;17E in monocytes (green), GATA2+ macrophages (GATA2, magenta) and moMacs (orange). Data are expressed as RMA normalized expression levels. <bold>F</bold>, Expression of GATA2 in (moMacs) differentiated in the presence of the indicated concentration of PDGF&#x02010;AB/BB for 48&#x02009;hours. <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values are compared with 0&#x02009;ng/mL. <bold>G</bold>, Serum concentrations of total cholesterol, HDL, LDL, non&#x02010;HDL cholesterol, and total triglycerides in patients with atherosclerosis with high or low monocytic GATA2 expression. <bold>H</bold>, Expression of GATA2 in moMacs stimulated with the indicated concentrations of LDL for 3&#x02009;days. &#x00394;&#x00394;Ct values are normalized to the mean &#x00394;&#x00394;Ct of untreated moMacs (0&#x02009;mmol/L LDL). <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x02010;values are compared with 0&#x02009;mmol/L. n=3 (<bold>A</bold>, <bold>D</bold>), 5 (<bold>B&#x02010;C</bold>), 9 (<bold>E</bold>), or 4 (<bold>F</bold> through <bold>G</bold>) biological replicates, Kruskal&#x02013;Wallis test with Dunn correction (<bold>B</bold> through <bold>F</bold>), Dunnett&#x02019;s test (<bold>H</bold>), or Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test (G). Ctrl indicates Control; HDL, high&#x02010;density lipoprotein; IL, interleukin; LDL, low&#x02010;density lipoprotein; MFI, mean fluorescence intensity; moMacs, monocyte&#x02010;derived macrophages; n.d., not detected; PDGF, platelet&#x02010;derived growth factor; and RMA, robust multiarray average.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JAH3-14-e043646-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-5"/></fig></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0029"><title>
<styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">GATA2</styled-content> Correlates With Macrophage Proliferation Throughout Disease</title><p>Up to this point, all analyses of GATA2 expression in plaque macrophages were performed in early&#x02010;stage atheromas present in aortic biopsies acquired during CABG surgery. As such, despite these patients being at an advanced stage of disease, these analyses used early&#x02010;stage or arrested plaques (Stary scores of 1 to 2<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0046" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>46</sup>
</xref>) and therefore may not represent GATA2 expression patterns throughout plaque development. Moreover, the gene expression profile of these early lesion macrophages suggests that they are proliferating. As such, we used histological staining and fluorescence immunohistochemistry of human coronary vessels with plaque across a range of disease severity (Stary scores of 1 to 5) to identify macrophages (CD68+ cells), GATA2, and the cell proliferation marker Ki&#x02010;67 (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4A</xref>). Using a trained machine learning algorithm,<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0040" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>40</sup>
</xref> we identified the macrophages in each lesion and classified them based on their expression of GATA2 and Ki&#x02010;67 (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4B</xref>). Proliferating (Ki&#x02010;67+) cells, GATA2+ cells, and Ki&#x02010;67+/GATA2+ cells were enriched on the intimal side of the lesions, with proliferation in this region observed in both macrophages (CD68+) and in nonmacrophage (CD68&#x02010;) cell types (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4C</xref>). Macrophages were rarely observed outside of the lesional area, with these nonlesional macrophages lacking GATA2 expression (data not shown). It has been reported that a subpopulation of smooth muscle cells express CD68 during atherosclerosis, and that GATA2 can induce the differentiation of smooth muscle cells into macrophage&#x02010;like foam cells.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0047" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>47</sup>
</xref> As such, we stained sections of these same tissues for CD68, GATA2, and the smooth muscle cell marker SMA (smooth muscle actin; Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4D</xref>, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S4A</xref>). CD68 was predominantly expressed by macrophages, with &#x0003c;3% of CD68+ cells costaining with SMA (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4D</xref>). Interestingly, a larger portion of smooth muscle cells expressed GATA2 than did macrophages, but GATA2 expression was not concomitant with CD68 expression in these cells, confirming that &#x0003e;95% of the CD68+/GATA2+ cells in these samples were macrophages (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4D</xref>). There was no statistically significant correlation between lesion size and the portion of lesional cells that were macrophages (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4E</xref>), even after removal of the outlier (R<sup>2</sup>=0.1829, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>=0.2509)&#x02014;but there was a modest correlation between lesion size and macrophage density (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4F</xref>). Ten percent of cells in the atheroma were with macrophages accounting for 15% of the proliferating cells (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4G</xref>). There was a strong correlation between GATA2 expression and macrophage proliferation, with between 92% and 100% of proliferating macrophages expressing GATA2 (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4H</xref> and <xref rid="jah311546-fig-0004" ref-type="fig">4I</xref>). It was unclear whether this upregulation of GATA2 was specific to macrophages in the atheroma or whether this occurred in other tissues in these patients. To address this possibility, we recovered tissues that often are coinvolved in atherosclerosis (adipose tissue and liver) and a tissue that is minimally affected by atherosclerosis (skin) and stained these tissues for macrophages, GATA2, and Ki&#x02010;67 (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S4B</xref> through <xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S4E</xref>). Although a small portion of CD68+/GATA2+ cells were found in all 3 tissues, the relative abundance of these GATA2+ macrophages as a portion of total macrophages was lower in these tissues than in atherosclerotic plaque. Moreover, most proliferating macrophages in these tissues lacked GATA2 expression, suggesting that the association of GATA2 with macrophage proliferation is specific to the atheroma microenvironment (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S4E</xref>). These data indicate that GATA2 expression is concomitant with macrophage proliferation in atheromas, although it is unclear if GATA2 is driving proliferation or if GATA2 expression is a product of proliferation.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="Figure" id="jah311546-fig-0004"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><title>GATA2 is correlated with macrophage proliferation in advanced atherosclerotic plaque.</title><p>Fluorescence histology of human coronary blood vessels was used to identify proliferating cells using the marker Ki&#x02010;67, macrophages with the marker CD68+, and GATA2. <bold>A</bold>, Fluorescent labeling of a human coronary blood vessel containing extensive atherosclerotic plaque for DNA (blue), macrophages/CD68 (cyan), GATA2 (magenta) and Ki&#x02010;67 (yellow). Scale bar is 500&#x02009;&#x003bc;m, boxed area indicates a lesional area shown in higher magnification in (<bold>B</bold>). <bold>B</bold>, Magnified area of the region of interest from (<bold>A</bold>). Left: fluorescent labeling for DNA (blue), macrophages/CD68 (cyan), GATA2 (magenta), and Ki&#x02010;67 (yellow). Right: Automated classification (detections) of all cells in region of interest from (<bold>A</bold>). Cells are classified based on their expression of CD68 (macrophages), Ki&#x02010;67, and GATA2. Double&#x02010;negative M&#x003c6; are in cyan, GATA2+/ki67+ macrophages (M&#x003c6;&#x02010;GATA2&#x02010;Ki&#x02010;67) are in yellow; no single&#x02010;positive macrophages are present. Nonmacrophages (CD68&#x02010; cells) expressing GATA2 (magenta), Ki&#x02010;67 (green), both GATA2 and Ki&#x02010;67 (orange), or neither marker (red) are also indicated. <bold>C</bold>, Position of cells within advanced lesions, categorized as per the classification scheme from (<bold>B</bold>). Position is measured as the cells distance along a unit vector connecting each cell to the nearest intimal and luminal edges of the lesion. A total of 4863 cells, detected in 6 independent histological sections, are plotted. <bold>D</bold>, Quantification of GATA2 expression patterns in macrophages (CD68+/SMA&#x02212;), smooth muscle cells (CD68&#x02010;/SMA+), and smooth muscle cell&#x02010;derived foam cells (CD68+/SMA+) in advanced plaque. <bold>E</bold>, There is no correlation between the portion of the blood vessel containing atherosclerotic plaque (lesional involvement) and the fraction of cells in the lesions that are macrophages (CD68+ cells). <bold>F</bold>, The density of macrophages in a plaque is positively correlated with the portion of the blood vessel containing atherosclerotic plaque (lesional involvement). <bold>G</bold>, Quantification of the portion of dividing cells (Ki&#x02010;67+ cells) within lesions that are macrophages (CD68+ cells) vs other cell types (CD68&#x02212; cell types). <bold>H</bold>, Quantification of the fraction of macrophages in atherosclerotic lesions that express GATA2, ki&#x02010;67, or both markers. <bold>I</bold>, Portion of proliferating macrophages that lack (&#x02212;) or express (+) GATA2. n&#x02009;=&#x02009;3863 cells quantified across 9 biological replicates (<bold>C</bold>), 3 biological replicates (<bold>D</bold>), or 9 biological replicates (<bold>E</bold> through <bold>I</bold>), Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test (<bold>E</bold>, <bold>G</bold>) or Kruskal&#x02013;Wallis test with Dunn correction (<bold>F</bold>). Correlative <italic toggle="yes">R</italic>
<sup>2</sup> and <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values were calculated using a Spearman&#x02019;s correlation coefficient. Fluorescent images in (<bold>A</bold>) and (<bold>B</bold>) have had their intensity square root&#x02010;normalized to better illustrate the distribution of cells within the images. M&#x003c6; indicates macrophage; ROI, region of interest; and SMA, smooth muscle actin.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JAH3-14-e043646-g002" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-7"/></fig></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0030"><title>
<styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">GATA2</styled-content> Is Required for Macrophage Proliferation</title><p>To investigate the role of GATA2 in macrophage proliferation, we conducted a network analysis to identify genes associated with cell proliferation that are expressed by GATA2+ macrophages. We restricted this network to proteins that physically interact with GATA2 or that interact with GATA2&#x02010;interacting proteins. This analysis revealed a potential network regulating proliferation in these cells (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5A</xref>). This network was reminiscent of the same proliferative network mediated by GATA2 during myelopoiesis, particularly in the upregulation of the protooncogene MYB and the downregulation of JUN.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref> We used GATA2 overexpressing human THP1 monocytes to investigate the impact of GATA2 expression on this putative pathway, allowing us to investigate GATA2&#x02019;s role in the absence of additional signaling induced by oxLDL or other plaque&#x02010;derived signals. Consistent with the network analysis, GATA2 overexpression upregulated MYB and reduced expression of JUN (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5B</xref>). We next used a luciferase reporter assay to determine if GATA2 was directly regulating expression of JUN and MYB during macrophage differentiation. As expected, GATA2 suppressed JUN promotor activity, but GATA2 expression did not alter MYB promoter activity (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5C</xref> and <xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5D</xref>), indicating that GATA2 directly regulates the JUN promoter but may be regulating MYB expression via distal regulatory elements not included in our luciferase reporters.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0049" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>49</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>50</sup>
</xref> Consistent with GATA2 regulating MYB via distal regulatory elements, ChIP&#x02010;PCR identified GATA2&#x02010;bound regions at the &#x02212;34 kb and&#x02009;&#x02212;&#x02009;68 kb enhancer regions of MYB but not in the core MYB promoter (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S5A</xref>). MYB was expressed in THP1 monocytes and THP1&#x02010;derived macrophages in a GATA2&#x02010;dependent manner, with monocytic expression further increased by GATA2 overexpression (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5E</xref>). Although MYB expression was dependent on GATA2, knockdown of MYB did not alter basal GATA2 expression but did lead to higher GATA2 expression following oxLDL stimulation (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S5B</xref>). These data suggest that GATA2 expression induces MYB, with MYB then providing negative feedback that limits the expression of GATA2.</p><fig position="float" fig-type="Figure" id="jah311546-fig-0005"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><title>GATA2 drives macrophage proliferation.</title><p>
<bold>A</bold>, Cytoscape network analysis identified enrichment of a proliferative network in the transcriptome of GATA2+ macrophages compared with monocytes from healthy controls. <bold>B</bold>, Overexpression of GATA2 in THP1&#x02010;derived macrophages increases the expression of MYB and decreases the expression of JUN compared with wild&#x02010;type THP1 macrophages. <bold>C</bold> and <bold>D</bold>, Quantification of JUN (<bold>C</bold>) and MYB (<bold>D</bold>) promoter activity using firefly luciferase reporters containing the MYB or JUN promoters, in WT vs GATA2&#x02010;overexpressing (GATA2) THP1&#x02010;derived macrophages. Cells are either untransfected (Mock), transfected with a promoterless vector (Empty) or transfected with a MYB&#x02010; or JUN&#x02010;promoter containing vector. <bold>E</bold>, MYB expression measured by RT&#x02010;PCR in WT, GATA2&#x02010;overexpressing (GATA2), and GATA2&#x02010;shRNA expression (shGATA2) THP1 monocytes and THP1&#x02010;derived macrophages. <bold>F</bold>, Impact of GATA2 on the proliferation rate of GATA2&#x02010;overexpressing (GATA2) vs WT THP1 monocytes as they differentiate into macrophages. <bold>G</bold>, GATA2 expression in THP1&#x02010;macrophages following exposure to the indicated concentrations of oxLDL for 48&#x02009;hours. <italic toggle="yes">P</italic> values are in comparison to 0&#x02009;mmol/L. <bold>H</bold>, Impact of GATA2 and MYB siRNA knockdown, vs a scrambled siRNA control (Scrm), on the proliferation of THP1&#x02010;macrophages that are untreated (UT) or treated with oxLDL. <bold>I</bold>, THP1 proliferation during monocyte&#x02013;macrophage differentiation and during macrophage&#x02010;foam cell differentiation. THP1 cells are either WT or GATA2 overexpressing (GATA2)&#x000b1;treatment with oxLDL during monocyte&#x02010;to&#x02010;macrophage differentiation (oxLDL), or were first differentiated fully into macrophages before loading with proliferation dye, and proliferation quantified during FC differentiation. Data are presented as the integrated cell proliferation dye intensity of &#x0003e;250 cells. Proliferation is identified as a decrease in the intensity of the cell proliferation dye compared with the fixed control sample (Fixed). *<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003c;0.0001, <sup>&#x02020;</sup>
<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>&#x0003e;0.9999 between indicated groups. <bold>J</bold>, RT&#x02010;PCR quantification of GATA2 and MYB expression during the polarization of (HC) vs Pt monocytes into M&#x003c6; and FC. <bold>K</bold>, THP1&#x02010;derived macrophage apoptosis under control conditions vs following stimulation with 2.5&#x02009;mmol/L oxLDL, or 10&#x02009;&#x003bc;mol/L Tg. Macrophages are either derived from WT THP1 cells (green), GATA2&#x02010;overexpressing THP1 cells (magenta), or THP1 cells expressing a GATA2&#x02010;depleating shRNA (shGATA2, orange). n=3 (<bold>B</bold>, <bold>E</bold>, <bold>I</bold>), 5 (<bold>C</bold>, <bold>D</bold>, <bold>H</bold>, <bold>K</bold>), 6 (<bold>F</bold>), or 4 (<bold>G</bold>, <bold>J</bold>) biological replicates, Mann&#x02013;Whitney <italic toggle="yes">U</italic> test (<bold>B</bold> through <bold>D</bold>, <bold>H</bold>) or Kruskal&#x02013;Wallis test with Dunn correction (<bold>G</bold>, <bold>I</bold>, <bold>J</bold>). FC indicates foam cell; HC, healthy control; M&#x003c6;, macrophage; oxLDL, oxidized low&#x02010;density lipoprotein; Pt, Patient; RT&#x02010;PCR, reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction; Tg, thapsigargin; and WT, wild&#x02010;type.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JAH3-14-e043646-g004" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-9"/></fig><p>Next, the impact of GATA2 expression on proliferation was quantified using a dye&#x02010;dilution assay, with GATA2 overexpressing cells proliferating significantly more than wild&#x02010;type cells (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5F</xref>, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S5C</xref> through <xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S5E</xref>). This indicates that GATA2 expression is sufficient to drive macrophage proliferation, but it is unclear whether this occurs under atherogenic conditions. GATA2 was upregulated in oxLDL&#x02010;stimulated THP1 macrophages (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5G</xref>), with oxLDL stimulation inducing proliferation of these cells during macrophage differentiation (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5H</xref>). Critically, this proliferation was suppressed in THP1 cells expressing a GATA2&#x02010;targeting shRNA or when THP1 cells were treated with a cell&#x02010;penetrant siRNA against MYB (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5H</xref>, Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S5F</xref>). To determine when proliferation was occurring, we used dye dilution to quantify the proliferation of monocytic THP1 cells undergoing differentiation into macrophages and in differentiated THP1 macrophages undergoing oxLDL&#x02010;induced foam cell formation. In differentiating monocytes, oxLDL and GATA2 overexpression induced additional proliferation compared with controls, with no additive effect observed when GATA2 overexpressing cells were stimulated with oxLDL (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5I</xref>). Analysis of the morphological changes that occur when monocytes differentiate into macrophages also failed to identify any impact of GATA2&#x02010;overexpression on the rate of macrophage differentiation or on the final morphology of the differentiated cells (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S5G</xref> and <xref rid="jah311546-supitem-0001" ref-type="supplementary-material">S5H</xref>). In contrast, differentiated macrophages did not proliferate when foam cell formation was induced, even when GATA2 was overexpressed (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5I</xref>). To confirm that this was not a cell line artifact, GATA2 and MYB expression were quantified in healthy control and patient&#x02010;derived monocytes during differentiation into macrophages and foam cells. As with the THP1 cells, GATA2 and MYB were elevated during monocyte&#x02010;to&#x02010;macrophage differentiation, with expression of both genes decreasing when mature macrophages were differentiated into foam cells&#x02014;with elevated levels of both GATA2 and MYB observed in patient cells at all time points (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5J</xref>). Lipid&#x02010;laden macrophages often undergo apoptosis due to activation of the unfolded protein response triggered by the intracellular accumulation of sterols and lipids. Our gene expression analysis of the GATA2+ macrophage transcriptome identified upregulation of several antiapoptotic genes (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1G</xref>), suggesting that GATA2 expression may desensitize macrophages to apoptosis. Although we did not observe apoptosis in response to oxLDL, GATA2 overexpression reduced apoptosis, and GATA2 knockdown increased apoptosis, following induction of the unfolded protein response with thapsigargin (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="jah311546-fig-0005" ref-type="fig">5K</xref>).<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0051" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>51</sup>
</xref> Combined, these results indicate that GATA2 is expressed in response to oxLDL, thereby promoting atheroma development by inducing macrophage proliferation and desensitizing these cells to apoptotic stressors present in the atheroma.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion" id="jah311546-sec-0031"><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>Macrophage proliferation produces the majority of macrophages present in an atherosclerotic plaque, suggesting that reducing macrophage proliferation could be a potentially powerful therapeutic approach.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>10</sup>
</xref> Using human aortic biopsies and coronary artery sections, we identified the hematopoietic transcription factor GATA2 as a protein uniquely expressed in proliferating plaque macrophages. These GATA2+ macrophages had a transcriptional profile with characteristics of both monocytes and macrophages, indicating that these cells were either arrested at a midpoint in monocyte differentiation or were macrophages that had partially dedifferentiated to an immature state. These cells expressed the proliferation marker Ki&#x02010;67, and in&#x000a0;vitro, GATA2 expression was necessary and sufficient to induce macrophage proliferation, mediating oxLDL&#x02010;induced proliferation by upregulation of the protooncogene MYB. Together, these results lead us to conclude that oxLDL&#x02010;induced GATA2 expression in atherosclerotic plaque macrophages induces macrophage proliferation via a GATA2&#x02010;MYB mediated proliferative network. Thus, GATA2 or its downstream effectors may be viable therapeutic targets for the treatment of atherosclerosis, given that the bulk of macrophages in the plaque arise via proliferation.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>9</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>Previous studies have linked single nucleotide variants (SNVs) in GATA2 to atherosclerosis, although there does not appear to be a consistent mechanism in how different GATA2 SNVs contribute to disease. Muiya et&#x000a0;al. identified 8 SNVs in GATA2 linked to coronary artery disease, with broadly different impacts on disease risk factors and disease progression. This included identification of SNVs associated with LDL levels, type 2 diabetes, obesity, hypercholesterolemia, and predisposition to myocardial infarct without impacts on other prognostic markers.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref> Only 1 of these SNVs affected the coding region of GATA2 (rs2335052), with the remainder located in the intron between exons 5 and 6, in the 3&#x02032; UTR, or in the nontranscribed region 3&#x02032; to the GATA2 gene. This coding sequence SNV results in an Ala<sup>164</sup>Thr mutation in the negative regulatory domain of GATA2, where it is associated with increased GATA2 proteins levels and with an increased risk of coronary artery disease and some cancers.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0053" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>53</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0054" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>54</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0055" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>55</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0056" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>56</sup>
</xref> Muiya et&#x000a0;al. also identified a common frameshift mutation that results in the haploid expression of GATA2, but no link to atherosclerosis was found even though the same mutation predisposes people to leukemia.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0052" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>52</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0057" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>57</sup>
</xref> Other mutations that decrease GATA2 expression, such as those causing MonoMac syndrome, are also not associated with atherosclerosis.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0058" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>58</sup>
</xref> GATA2 activity and expression levels can also be controlled via posttranslational modifications. For example, GATA2 phosphorylation reduces the nuclear localization of the GATA2 protein, with adipocytes expressing a form of GATA2 that cannot be phosphorylated (eg, constitutively nuclear localized) producing elevated levels of the proatherogenic cytokines MCP&#x02010;1 (MCP&#x02010;1) and GM&#x02010;CSF.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0059" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>59</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0060" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>60</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0061" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>61</sup>
</xref> Although the mechanisms by which these various SNVs and pathways drive atherosclerosis are unclear, these studies consistently show that transcriptional and posttranslational modifications that increase GATA2 expression or activity are atherogenic, whereas no linkage is observed between atherosclerosis and processes that reduce GATA2 expression or activity. These findings are consistent with our observations, wherein elevated GATA2 expression drove proatherogenic changes in macrophage proliferation and decreased cells sensitivity to apoptosis.</p><p>In adults, GATA2 expression is generally limited to 2 sites: lymphatic endothelium and hematopoietic stem cells and myeloid progenitors in the bone marrow.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0062" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>62</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0063" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>63</sup>
</xref> In both locations, GATA2 is critical for self&#x02010;renewal, with GATA2 expression driven by cyclin&#x02010;dependent kinases.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref> GATA2 expression is self&#x02010;reenforcing via a positive feedback loop in which GATA2 activates its own promoter.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0064" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>64</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0065" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>65</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0066" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>66</sup>
</xref> In the bone marrow, GATA2 expression must be reduced for myeloid cells to enter terminal differentiation. This cessation of GATA2 expression is mediated by a GATA2/GATA1 switch, wherein upregulation of GATA1 displaces GATA2 from its binding sites in the GATA2 promoter, leading to decreased GATA2 expression.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0066" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>66</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0067" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>67</sup>
</xref> Other than our initial identification of GATA2 in atherosclerotic plaque macrophages, there are no reports of human GATA2 expression in noncancerous peripheral myeloid cells.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>18</sup>
</xref> Indeed, the only report of GATA2 expression in mature macrophages is in the alveolar macrophages of rats chronically infected with the fungal pathogen <italic toggle="yes">Pneumocystis carinii</italic>, where GATA2 expression impairs macrophage phagocytosis.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0068" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>68</sup>
</xref> In mouse macrophages, toll&#x02010;like receptor 4 signaling, triggered by lipopolysaccharide, promotes GATA2 expression, which then upregulates expression of the inflammatory cytokine IL&#x02010;1&#x003b2;.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0069" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>69</sup>
</xref> These results suggest that GATA2 expression in peripheral macrophages is an aberrant state, perhaps brought on by persistent inflammation or other nonresolving immunological stimuli. Although not detected in myeloid cells, human lung endothelial cells upregulate GATA2 in response to acute infection with SARS&#x02010;CoV&#x02010;2 where it contributes to the production of inflammatory cytokines, again consistent with GATA2 expression being induced by inflammation.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0070" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>70</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0071" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>71</sup>
</xref> GATA2 expression is also induced by cell stress, with increased activity of the GATA2 promoter observed after heat stress, and GATA2 expression promotes reendothelialization of injured carotid arteries.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0050" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>50</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0072" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>72</sup>
</xref> Our observation that oxLDL, but not LDL, potently induced GATA2 expression in macrophages is consistent with both of these processes as oxLDL, but not LDL, induces inflammatory signaling via CD36 and toll like receptors 2 and 4, as well as provokes cell stress via its intracellular accumulation and activation of the unfolded protein response.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>4</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0073" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>73</sup>
</xref>
</p><p>We found that oxLDL&#x02010;induced proliferation required both GATA2 and MYB, which is consistent with previous studies that identified MYB as a transcriptional target of GATA2, with GATA2&#x02010;induced MYB expression mediating the transition of cells from G<sub>1</sub> to S phase.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref> Although we saw a significant induction of MYB mRNA in cells overexpressing GATA2, and a decrease in MYB expression in cells where GATA2 was depleted, we did not see a concurrent increase in MYB promoter activity. This was not unexpected as GATA2 and other GATA transcription factors regulate MYB largely via distal promoter elements&#x02014;regions not captured in our luciferase vector.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0032" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>32</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0074" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>74</sup>
</xref> Indeed, previous ChIPseq analysis of myeloid cells and erythroid&#x02010;myeloid progenitor cells identified a GATA2 binding region within the &#x02212;34 kb and&#x02009;&#x02212;&#x02009;68 kb MYB enhancers (Gene Expression Omnibus GSM1488757 and GSM935373), and we identified GATA2 binding to these enhancers in GATA2&#x02010;overexpressing THP1 macrophages. Although GATA2 was required for the oxLDL&#x02010;mediated induction of MYB expression, once expressed, MYB negatively regulated GATA2 expression. This is consistent with previous studies demonstrating the suppression of multiple genes, including GATA2, when MYB interacts with members of the C/EBP family.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0075" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>75</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0076" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>76</sup>
</xref> We also observed downregulation of JUN promoter activity and mRNA levels. Although JUN can mediate progression through G<sub>1</sub> phase, via regulating cyclin D1 levels, JUN is antagonistic to GATA2 activity.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0048" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>48</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0077" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>77</sup>
</xref> Although little research has been done into the regulation of JUN by GATA&#x02010;family transcription factors, ChIP&#x02010;seq analyses have identified a site bound by GATA2, GATA3, GATA4 and GATA6 in the JUN promoter, consisting of a canonical AGATAA binding motif, located roughly 60 base&#x02010;pairs 5&#x02032; to JUN&#x02019;s transcriptional start site.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0078" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>78</sup>
</xref> Although the mechanism by which GATA2 suppresses JUN expression is not clear from our data, GATA transcription factors are known to engage in &#x0201c;switch&#x0201d; behavior, where GATAs that bind to the same site displace each other in a competitive manner that is dependent on the relative affinity for the binding site and the relative abundance of the GATA proteins.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0079" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>79</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0080" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>80</sup>
</xref>, <xref rid="jah311546-bib-0081" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>81</sup>
</xref> Thus, increased expression of GATA2 could displace a normally activating GATA at this site, with GATA2 then negatively regulating the expression of JUN. Given that GATA2 expression is lowest at the start of G<sub>1</sub> phase and increases as cells enter the G<sub>1</sub>/S transition and pass through to G<sub>2</sub> phase, these data indicate that GATA2 and JUN are upregulated at different points of the cell cycle, with cross&#x02010;talk between these genes controlling both their mutual expression, and controlling cell cycle progression.<xref rid="jah311546-bib-0031" ref-type="bibr">
<sup>31</sup>
</xref>
</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions" id="jah311546-sec-0032"><title>CONCLUSIONS</title><p>In summary, we have identified GATA2 as a transcription factor upregulated in a subpopulation of macrophages present throughout atheroma development. These GATA2+ cells feature a transcriptome midway between monocytes and fully differentiated macrophages and constitute the primary replicating macrophage population within the plaque. In these cells, GATA2 induces expression of MYB, initiating a GATA2/MYB&#x02010;dependent proliferation pathway. As differentiation progresses, negative feedback from MYB suppresses GATA2 expression, leading to the cessation of proliferation and the completion of macrophages maturation. Given that most macrophages present in atherosclerotic plaque arise via macrophage proliferation, this GATA2&#x02010;mediated pathway accounts for much of the cellularity of plaques and represents a potential therapeutic target for reducing plaque burden and inducing plaque regression.</p></sec><sec id="jah311546-sec-0034"><title>Sources of Funding</title><p>This study was funded by a Heart and Stroke Foundation of Canada Grant&#x02010;In&#x02010;Aid to Bryan Heit. Amena Aktar was funded by an Ontario Graduate Scholarship from the Government of Ontario, and by a Dr Robert George Everitt Murray Graduate Scholarship from The University of Western Ontario. PCD was funded via the X&#x02010;Labs program at The University of Western Ontario. The funders had no role in study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish, or preparation of the article.</p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="jah311546-sec-0035"><title>Disclosures</title><p>None.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supporting information</title><supplementary-material id="jah311546-supitem-0001" position="float" content-type="local-data"><caption><p>Tables S1&#x02013;S4</p><p>Figures&#x000a0;S1&#x02013;S5</p></caption><media xlink:href="JAH3-14-e043646-s001.pdf"/></supplementary-material></sec></body><back><ack id="jah311546-sec-0033"><title>Acknowledgments</title><p>We would like to thank the Molecular Imaging Facility (<ext-link xlink:href="http://www.molimage.ca" ext-link-type="uri">www.molimage.ca</ext-link>) and the London Regional Flow Cytometry Facility for their assistance with the microscopy and flow cytometry assays.</p></ack><ref-list content-type="cited-references" id="jah311546-bibl-0001"><title>References</title><ref id="jah311546-bib-0001"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal" id="jah311546-cit-0001">
